USAGE: pono [options] <btor file>

Options:
  --help                                 
                    Print usage and exit.
  --engine, -e <engine>                  
                    Select engine from [bmc, bmc-sp, ind, interp, mbic3,
                    ic3bits, ic3ia, msat-ic3ia, ic3sa, sygus-pdr].
  --bound, -k                            
                    Bound to check up until (default: 10).
  --prop, -p                             
                    Property index to check (default: 0).
  --verbosity, -v                        
                    Verbosity for printing to standard out.
  --random-seed,                         
                    Random seed.
  --vcd                                  
                    Name of Value Change Dump (VCD) if witness exists.
  --smt-solver                           
                    SMT Solver to use: btor, msat, or cvc5.
  --logging-smt-solver                   
                    Use Smt-Switch logging solver which guarantees the exact
                    term structure that was created. Good for avoiding term
                    rewriting at the API level or sort aliasing. (default:
                    false)
  --witness                              
                    Print witness if the property is false.
  --static-coi                           
                    Apply static (i.e., one-time before solving)
                    cone-of-influence analysis.
  --show-invar                           
                    For engines that produce invariants, show the invariant
  --check-invar                          
                    For engines that produce invariants, check that they hold.
  --reset, -r <reset input>              
                    Symbol to use for reset signal (prefix with ~ for negative
                    reset)
  --resetsteps, -s <integer>             
                    Number of steps to apply reset for (default: 1)
  --clock, -c <clock name>               
                    Symbol to use for clock signal (only supports starting at 0
                    and toggling each step)
  --ic3-no-pregen                        
                    Disable preimage generalization in ic3.
  --ic3-no-indgen                        
                    Disable inductive generalization in ic3.
  --ic3-gen-max-iter                     
                    Max number of iterations (greater than zero) for
                    unsatcore-based ic3 generalization. Setting it to 0 means an
                    unbounded number of iterations.
  --ic3-functional-preimage              
                    Use functional preimage in ic3.
  --no-ic3-unsatcore-gen                 
                    Disable unsat core generalization during relative induction
                    check. That extra generalization helps several IC3 variants
                    but also runs the risk of myopic over-generalization. Some
                    IC3 variants have better inductive generalization and do
                    better with this option.
  --no-ic3ia-reduce-preds                
                    Disable unsat core based predicate minimization
  --no-ic3ia-track-important-vars        
                    Ignore tracked important variables when picking predicates.
  --no-ic3sa-func-refine                 
                    Disable functional unrolling attempt  in IC3SA.
  --mbic3-indgen-mode                    
                    ModelBasedIC3 inductive generalization mode [0,2].
                                         
                    0 - normal, 1 - embedded init constraint, 2 - interpolation.
  --profiling-log                        
                    Name of logfile for profiling output (requires build with
                    linked profiling library 'gperftools').
  --pseudo-init-prop                     
                    Replace init and prop with state variables -- can extend
                    trace by up to two steps. Recommended for use with ic3ia.
                    Important note: will promote system to be relational
  --assume-prop                          
                    enable assuming property in pre-state (default disabled)
  --ceg-prophecy-arrays                  
                    Use counter-example guided prophecy for arrays.
  --no-cegp-timed-axiom-red              
                    Don't reduce enumerated axioms in CEG-Prophecy with unsat
                    cores.
  --no-cegp-consec-axiom-red             
                    Don't reduce consecutive axioms in CEG-Prophecy before
                    adding to transition system.
  --no-cegp-nonconsec-axiom-red          
                    Don't reduce non-consecutive axioms in CEG-Prophecy before
                    creating prophecy variables.
  --cegp-force-restart                   
                    Force underlying engine to restart after refinement.
  --cegp-abs-vals                        
                    abstract values in ceg-prophecy-arrays (only supported for
                    IC3IA)
  --cegp-abs-vals-cutoff                 
                    cutoff value for what to abstract - must be positive
                    (default: 100)
  --cegp-strong-abs                      
                    Use strong abstraction in CEGP -- no equality UFs (default:
                    false)
  --ceg-bv-arith                         
                    abstraction-refinement for the BV arithmetic operators (mul,
                    div, rem, mod). (only supported for IC3IA)
  --ceg-bv-arith-min-bw                  
                    minimum bitwidth of operators to abstract - must be positive
                    (default: 16) 
  --promote-inputvars                    
                    promote all input variables to state variables
  --sygus-op-lv                          
                    operator abstraction level (0-2, default:0) (only for SYGUS
                    PDR)
  --sygus-term-mode                      
                    term generation mode (0-4, default: 4 AUTO) (0: more
                    replace, 1: v/c ext 2: v/c split 3: v/c lt/le )
  --ic3sa-initial-terms-lvl              
                    Configures where to find terms for the initial abstraction.
                    Higher numbers means more terms and predicates will be
                    included in the inital abstraction [0-4] (default: 4).
  --ic3sa-interp                         
                    use interpolants to find more terms during refinement
                    (default: off)
  --print-wall-time                      
                    Print wall clock time of entire execution
  --bmc-bound-start                      
                    Bound (unrolling depth) to start cex search in BMC (default:
                    0)
  --bmc-bound-step                       
                    Amount by which bound (unrolling depth) for cex search is
                    increased in BMC (default: 1). For values greater than 1,
                    BMC searches for cex in intervals of size
                    '--bmc-bound-step'.
  --bmc-neg-init-step                    
                    Add negated initial state constraint in BMC steps k > 0
                    (default: false).
  --bmc-exponential-step                 
                    Double BMC bound in each step starting at 'bmc-bound-start'
                    (default: false, explores bounds 0, 1, 2, 4,...).
  --bmc-single-bad-state                 
                    EXPERT OPTION: add a single bad state literal for current
                    bound k rather than a disjunctive term covering the checked
                    interval; counterexamples may be missed. (default: false).
  --bmc-neg-bad-step                     
                    Add negated bad state constraint in BMC steps k > 0
                    (default: false).
  --bmc-neg-bad-step-all                 
                    EXPERT OPTION: like '--bmc-neg-bad-step' but add negated bad
                    state constraint in ALL BMC steps k > 0 (default: false).
                    When combined with --bmc-single-bad-state, this option may
                    cause overconstraining the problem in certain corner cases.
  --bmc-min-cex-linear-search            
                    Apply linear instead of binary search for minimal cex after
                    a cex was found in current interval
  --bmc-min-cex-less-inc-bin-search      
                    Apply less incremental variant of binary search for minimal
                    cex after a cex was found in current interval
  --bmc-allow-non-minimal-cex            
                    Do not search for minimal cex within an interval;instead,
                    terminate immediately (reported bound of cex is an upper
                    bound of actual cex)
  --kind-no-simple-path-check            
                    Skip simple path check in k-induction (WARNING: might cause
                    incompleteness)
  --kind-eager-simple-path-check         
                    Eager simple path check in k-induction (default: lazy check)
  --kind-no-multi-call-simple-path-check 
                    Try to avoid multiple solver calls in lazy simple path check
                    in k-induction
  --kind-no-ind-check-init-states        
                    K-induction: skip checking inductive case based on initial
                    states
  --kind-no-ind-check                    
                    K-induction: skip inductive case checks; implies
                    '--kind-no-ind-check-init-states' and
                    '--kind-no-ind-check-property' (WARNING: will cause
                    incompleteness on most problem instances)
  --kind-no-ind-check-property           
                    K-induction: skip checking inductive case based on property
                    (WARNING: will cause incompleteness on most problem
                    instances)
  --kind-one-time-base-check             
                    K-induction: check base case only once after inductive check
                    was unsatisfiable (WARNING: counterexamples might be missed)
  --kind-bound-step                      
                    Amount by which bound (unrolling depth) is increased in
                    k-induction (default: 1)
